  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH NEXT MONDAY/...  \nThe initial ridge Tuesday will be the starting point for a \npersistent anti-cyclonic setup through Thanksgiving and the ensuing \nweekend. An unseasonably strong area of high pressure is shown \nbuilding to the west of the Baja Peninsula and expanding across \nmuch of the western CONUS during the back-half of the week. This \nwill keep mostly quiet conditions in the forecast during the \nextended period, resulting in what looks to be a wonderful holiday\ntimeframe weather-wise. Another round of breeziness Thursday, \nthis time focused over the lower Colorado River Valley, will once \nagain be the deviant factor to the overall benign setup as the \nstrong high pressure interacts with the previously mentioned \nshortwave that will be over Northern Mexico by this point in time.\n\nAfternoon highs by Thanksgiving will rise far enough to be \ncomfortably above normals values for the back-end of November, but \nthat will only result in middle to upper 70s becoming more common \nacross lower elevation areas. There might be a few spots, mainly \nout in SW Arizona and SE California, that reach into the lower 80s\nby the weekend due to their closer proximity to the greatest \nheight anomalies, but that appears to be the upper limit of how \nfar temperatures will rise. With the high centered to our \nsouthwest, cool northerly flow will likely be an inhibiting factor\nas to why we will not see warmth that is completely out of the \nordinary for this time of year.",
